  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Social dialogue, workplace cooperation, collective bargaining and disputes resolution expanded and capacities developed</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The CPO KHM126 addresses the need for inclusive labour policies to support Cambodia&#x2019;s ambition of becoming an upper-middle-income country by 2030. While the government&#x2019;s Pentagonal Strategy and NSDP 2024&#x2013;2028 emphasize productivity, infrastructure, and investment, weak labour relations frameworks could exacerbate inequality and destabilize society. To mitigate this, the CPO promotes a decent work-driven development approach based on freedom of association, collective bargaining, and effective social dialogue. In the 2024&#x2013;2025 biennium, the CPO focuses on improving tripartite and bipartite consultation mechanisms to develop inclusive labour and social policies. It also promotes workplace cooperation and more efficient grievance and dispute resolution systems to ensure that growth and decent work go hand in hand. Aligned with DWCP Outcome 3.1, the CPO aims to strengthen gender-responsive social dialogue and institutional capacity among tripartite constituents. A key focus is improving Cambodia&#x2019;s minimum wage system. While current regulations apply only to the textile, garment, footwear, and travel goods sectors, the government has expressed interest in expanding this to other sectors. The ILO supported a feasibility study on the potential impacts of such an expansion, presenting findings to the National Council for Minimum Wage in April 2024. These discussions led to a decision to raise the minimum wage to USD 208 per month under Prakas 211 in September 2024. The primary beneficiaries are workers in wage-earning sectors, national wage-setting institutions, and employers involved in social dialogue. The strategy is implemented in collaboration with national authorities, workers&#x2019; and employers&#x2019; organizations, and international development partners, reinforcing a rights-based approach to sustainable economic growth.</narrative>
  </description>
